><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251029132847+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="79acb5be-f57d-4612-975e-3436eed076a6" date="D:20251029132847+10'00'" name="c8cda950-8951-44d0-b3ab-ddde7a46e8c8" icon="Comment" page="0" rect="53.702820,295.647675,77.702820,319.647675" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14b: there was no clear practical reflections on the outcomes of the study</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="612.283020,227.647675,796.283020,319.647675"
/></text
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251029132850+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="79acb5be-f57d-4612-975e-3436eed076a6" date="D:20251029132850+10'00'" name="b92bd718-fac3-465f-9b1c-44841e98385c" icon="Comment" page="0" rect="53.702820,295.647675,77.702820,319.647675" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Sports nutrition research should address the important questions that athletes and coaches need to consider to make evidence-based decisions about if and how to use a specific supplement. Medical supplements and sports foods can be chosen according to well-supported infor- mation about nutrient needs and nutritional goals. Unfortunately, the opportunity to undertake high qual- ity investigations of performance supplements is dwar- fed by the number of products on the market (even when these are limited to those with some evidence base) and the complexities of real-life scenarios of use that might make a difference to performance outcomes. Neverthe- less, models to conduct and interpret studies of perfor- mance supplements are available. Caffeine provides a handy illustration of the way that knowledge and prac- tice evolve in sports nutrition, with the likelihood that there is always something new or better to learn.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="612.283020,227.647675,796.283020,319.647675"
/></text
